            <WaterConservation LowFlushToilets="2" LowFlushToiletsUpgrade="2" />
            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

&lt;b&gt;Observed Air Leakage:&lt;/b&gt;

During the blower door test, there were various areas where excessive amounts of air leakage exist. The majority of the air leakage is coming through the chimney that vents the current hot water heater. There is also air leakage from the disconnected bathroom fan vent in the lower level, the 6" fresh air supply into the furnace return air ductwork, around the attic hatch, and around many of the older window frames, and even around the newer patio door frame.



As discussed, an upgrade to a new direct-vent, instantaneous condensing hot water heated would eliminate the need for the chimney, and reduce a significant amount of leakage. Reconnecting the bathroom fan vent pipe and perhaps partially closing the damper on the fresh air supply pipe (if you find the house has very little humidity in winter) will also help to reduce excessive air leakage and heat loss. Other longer term upgrades (such as wall insulation &amp; new windows) will help address other air leakage paths through the building envelope.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

For a 10% improvement you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 108&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;3.8 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 500 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must have a second home energy evaluation, where the air leakage reduction will be measured. For a full rebate, the rebate application, with a copy of the first page of both home energy evaluation reports, must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of the second energy evaluation. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

The original house from 1968 has a lower floor and upper floor. The lower floor exterior walls have a lower concrete wall section with 1.5" of extruded polystyrene insulation (R7.5) on the interior. There is an upper 2x4 (R12) pony wall on top of the concrete, that has had some additional interior insulation added along the left side and in the rear bedroom &amp; bathroom. On average, I estimate this pony wall to be about R20. The upper floor is the original 2x4 construction with R12 insulation. The front entrance addition in 2010 appears to have 2x6 exterior walls with R20 insulation.



I have calculated the estimated annual energy savings if there was an additional 3" (R15) of extruded polystyrene insulation added to the exterior of all the walls.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By adding a minimum R15 insulation to 100% of the exterior walls, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 936&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;33.2 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 1,875 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 0.7 (R-3.8). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website:

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



</mainWalls>
                <foundation>

INSULATE THE CONCRETE FLOOR



The lower level has a concrete floor that is essentially uninsulated - just some wood sheathing with carpet on top in the bedrooms. As there is a full 8' ceiling in the lower level, you could add 2" (R10) of extruded polystyrene insulation below the wood sheathing, without affecting head head of the occupants.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By adding R10 insulation to the concrete floor, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 170&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;6.0 GJ&lt;/b&gt;. There are currently no rebates available for adding insulation to concrete floors from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



</foundation>
                <windows>

I have modeled the potential energy savings associated with upgrading the 6 older wood windows to ENERGY STARÂ® qualified windows rated for climate zone 3. In addition, I recommend triple paned, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged windows. You mentioned that you may also be interested in upgrading the rear patio door to an ENERGY STARÂ® qualified Garden Door, and this is included as well.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt; 

By replacing the 6 windows and the patio door ENERGY STARÂ® qualified units rated for climate zone 3, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 316&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;11.2 GJ&lt;/b&gt;. There are currently no rebates available for installing new windows from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------</windows>
                <HotWaterSystem>

The current Bosch WR400-1K instantaneous hot water unit vents up a 5" chimney that is connected to the main chimney of the house (that the old furnace used to vent up through). The current unit has a standing pilot light, and draws all of its' combustion air from inside the house. I looked up the specs on the unit, which indicates an 82% thermal efficiency or a 0.69 energy factor. This unit allows a lot of heat loss from your house (up through the chimney), has wasted energy associated with the standing pilot and has a relatively low overall energy factor. I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STARÂ® qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with an energy factor of 0.92 or higher. This would be a direct vent model (no more chimney &amp; draws its' combustion air from outside the house, like the furnace does) and would not have a standing pilot (a waste of energy).



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By replacing your current hot water heater with an ENERGY STARÂ® qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with a thermal efficiency of 0.92, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 281&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, increase your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;10.0 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 1,250 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

</HotWaterSystem>
                <AdditionalComments>

&lt;b&gt;Using Biomass - Pellet Stove or a Wood Stove &lt;/b&gt;



Your home currently has a pellet stove on the upper floor that is direct vent (ie: it obtains combustion air through a dedicated pipe from the back of the stove to the outside). You mentioned that using the pellet stove creates good heat upstairs, however the lower level becomes quite cold. You also mentioned that you would like to install a woodstove perhaps downstairs (and harvest your own wood) however there is really no good location downstairs, as that level is divided into various bedrooms.



We discussed an option to have a high wall return air installed on the upper floor (right above where the current floor level return air is located) and having your furnace circulation fan (which has a high efficiency ECM motor) wired to operate intermittently (eg. 20 minute cycles on/off) to circulate your biomass heat from upstairs into the lower level rooms. Perhaps closing down some of the heat supplies on the upper level would help to push more heat downstairs.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

I have estimated that if you use your current pellet stove to provide 50% of the heating requirements of your house (at the bagged pellets rate listed at the bottom of this section), you would reduce your annual heating costs by $492 per year.



I have estimated that if you use install a wood stove and use it to provide 50% of the heating requirements of your house (at the self-harvested wood rate listed at the bottom of this section), you would reduce your annual heating costs by $1,078 per year.



If you decide to purchase &amp; install a new qualified wood stove, you could qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 750 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



&lt;b&gt;Ventilation&lt;/b&gt;



Your home is currently ventilated by using exhaust fans in the bathrooms (and the clothes dryer), where fresh air is drawn back into the house through a dampered fresh-air supply pipe that is connected to your furnace's return air ductwork. In addition your house has some natural air leakage that provides additional ventilation, and heat loss. Overall, your house appears to be slightly over-ventilated and could benefit from the reduced air leakage.



In addition, you may want to consider upgrading the bathroom fans to ENERGY STAR rated units connected to a humidistat, or a timer switch. This will help control excess moisture within the bathrooms, and the newer ENERGY STAR rated bathroom exhaust fans will incorporate a built-in damper to reduce any air leakage when it is not operating.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



&lt;b&gt;Depressurization Test&lt;/b&gt;



The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment (the kitchen range hood fan does not vent outside), 2 bathroom fans (the vent pipe on the lower level one has become disconnected), and the dryer, indicated a pressure drop of only 2.6 Pascals. As this number is lower than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment should not cause combustion products to be drawn into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances in your home that are not direct-vent. Your current furnace is a direct-vent model, and your pellet stove has a dedicated outdoor combustion air pipe connected, however your current instantaneous hot water unit is not direct-vent and could be susceptible to backdrafting fumes into the house if the depressrization level exceeds 5 Pacals.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



&lt;b&gt;Fuel Costs - Yellowknife&lt;/b&gt;



The following values have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Yellowknife:

Electricity - $19.45 min, $0.32 per kWh

Oil  - $1.12 per litre 

Propane - $0.71 per litre

Wood - $341 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested

Pellets - $5.67 per bag, $353.85 per tonne bulk delivered



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

